# Volute — Game state

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The build owns the shape of the game's state entirely. Choose the types, the
fields, and the modules that hold them to suit the implementation you write.

The one contract fixed over the state is the debugging and automation surface
specified for this build. Every operation that surface carries is a read or a
pose of the running game, so the state holds what those operations read, honors
what they write, and returns to its title values when the surface's `reset` is
called. Beyond satisfying that surface, how the state is organized is yours to
decide.

Whatever its shape, the state carries the following, because the rest of this
specification is written against them.

- The screen the game is showing, one of `title`, `playing`, `paused`,
  `cleared`, `setback`, `gameover`, or `victory`.
- The score, the level in play from `1` to `5`, and the cells remaining.
- The cores the inlet has yet to emit this level. How many it has emitted so far
  is the level's quota less that count, so no second counter is authoritative.
- The pressure, a real number from `0` to `100`.
- The chain step, at least `1`, and the seconds left before it returns to `1`.
- The timed machinery in force and the seconds it has left, or that none is in
  force.
- The train: an ordered list of the cores on the channel, head first, so arc
  positions descend along it. Each core carries its charge, its arc position
  from the inlet, and the machinery its mark grants or that it is unmarked.
- The segments: an ordered list, the lead segment first, each covering a count
  of consecutive cores of the train and carrying the seconds of recoil hold it
  has left. The counts partition the train in order.
- The projectiles in flight, oldest first, each with its charge, its center in
  logical units, and the heading in degrees it was fired along.
- The loaded charge and the queued charge, each a charge or nothing while no
  level is open, the injector's aim in degrees, and the seconds until the
  injector may fire again.
- The seconds left of the interlude the `cleared` and `setback` screens hold.
- The simulation time the run has accumulated, in seconds, which every update
  adds its elapsed time to whatever the screen.
- The frame time waiting for the next whole tick, at least `0` and below one
  tick. It grows on the screens that advance the simulation, and the delta time
  left unconsumed when the game stops advancing is discarded, so it is `0` on
  the screens that hold still.
- Whether the simulation advances on its own each frame.
- Whether the inlet emits, and whether the train advances. Both hold in play and
  neither is restored by a `reset` or a level opening: they are what the
  debugging surface's gates set, and they belong to the caller driving the game
  rather than to the run being played.
- Whether audio is muted. A fresh game starts with its audio unmuted, and the
  mute bit then belongs to the player: neither a `reset` nor a level opening
  moves it.
- The charge posed for the next core the inlet emits, or that none is posed. It
  is what the debugging surface's `setNextEmitted` sets, the emission that takes
  it clears it, and a `reset` clears it.

On `title` the run's fields hold their opening values: score `0`, level `1`,
three cells, the level's full quota still to emit, pressure `0`, chain step `1`,
no machinery, an empty channel, no projectiles, an aim of `270` degrees, and
every timer at `0`. The injector holds neither a loaded nor a queued charge
until a level opens. The `gameover` and `victory` screens keep the run that just
ended, since they report its score and level.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
`src/game.ts` declares and exports the state the game is written against. This
section is that declaration: write it into `src/game.ts` exactly as it stands
here. The three aliases `src/constants.ts` already exports are imported and
re-exported rather than declared a second time. The state is a value the engine
holds and every frame replaces, so every field and every array is declared
`readonly`: a frame builds the next state from the current one rather than
writing into it, and every reader is handed the state as
`DeepReadonly<VoluteState>` in any case.

```ts
import type { ChargeId, MachineryKind, ScreenName } from "./constants";

export type { ChargeId, MachineryKind, ScreenName };

export interface CoreState {
  readonly charge: ChargeId;
  readonly s: number;
  readonly mark: MachineryKind | null;
}

export interface SegmentState {
  readonly count: number;
  readonly hold: number;
}

export interface ProjectileState {
  readonly charge: ChargeId;
  readonly x: number;
  readonly y: number;
  readonly angle: number;
}

export interface MachineryState {
  readonly kind: MachineryKind;
  readonly remaining: number;
}

export interface VoluteState {
  readonly screen: ScreenName;

  readonly score: number;
  readonly level: number;
  readonly cells: number;
  readonly quotaRemaining: number;

  readonly pressure: number;
  readonly chainStep: number;
  readonly chainTimer: number;
  readonly machinery: MachineryState | null;

  readonly cores: readonly CoreState[];
  readonly segments: readonly SegmentState[];
  readonly projectiles: readonly ProjectileState[];

  readonly loaded: ChargeId | null;
  readonly queued: ChargeId | null;
  readonly aim: number;
  readonly fireCooldown: number;

  readonly interlude: number;
  readonly simTime: number;
  readonly accumulator: number;
  readonly emission: boolean;
  readonly feed: boolean;
  readonly nextEmitted: ChargeId | null;
  readonly muted: boolean;
}
```

## The type aliases

`ScreenName` is the top-level state machine, the seven members of `SCREENS`.
`ChargeId` names the five charges of `CHARGE_IDS`, and `MachineryKind` the four
kinds of `MACHINERY_KINDS`.

## `CoreState`

One core on the channel, in `cores`.

- `charge` — the charge it carries.
- `s` — its arc position, the distance from the inlet walked along `CHANNEL`,
  from `0` to `PATH_LENGTH` (`5000`).
- `mark` — the machinery extracting it grants, and `null` on an unmarked core.

## `SegmentState`

One segment, in `segments`.

- `count` — how many consecutive cores of `cores` the segment covers, at least
  `1`. The counts partition `cores` in order, so they sum to `cores.length` and
  the first segment is the lead segment.
- `hold` — the seconds of recoil hold the segment has left, `0` on a segment
  that advances.

## `ProjectileState`

One projectile, in `projectiles`.

- `charge` — the charge it carries and seats.
- `x`, `y` — its center, in logical units.
- `angle` — the heading it was fired along, in degrees, from `0` up to but not
  including `360`. It flies along that heading at `PROJECTILE_SPEED` (`620`)
  until it strikes or leaves the field.

## `MachineryState`

The timed machinery in force.

- `kind` — `choke`, `backflow`, or `sightline`.
- `remaining` — the seconds it has left, counting down to `0`, at which point
  `machinery` becomes `null`.

## `VoluteState`

The whole of Volute's state. Every field is present from the moment
`initialize` returns, and every one is plain data: numbers, strings, booleans,
`null`, and containers of them, so a scenario is posed by building a state that
differs in the fields posed and read back by reading them.

- `screen` — the screen currently shown.
- `score` — the run's score.
- `level` — the level in play, from `1` to `LEVEL_COUNT` (`5`).
- `cells` — the cells remaining, from `CELLS` (`3`) down to `0`.
- `quotaRemaining` — how many more cores the inlet emits this level. How many
  it has emitted so far this level is the level's `quota` less this value, so
  nothing else counts emissions.
- `pressure` — the pressure, from `PRESSURE_MIN` (`0`) to `PRESSURE_MAX`
  (`100`).
- `chainStep` — the chain step an extraction scores at, at least `1`.
- `chainTimer` — the seconds left before `chainStep` returns to `1`. It is
  `CHAIN_RESET` (`2.0`) on every extraction and falls with the ticks that
  advance play; it is `0` when no extraction stands inside its window.
- `machinery` — the timed machinery in force, and `null` when none is. `bore`
  resolves at once, so it never appears here.
- `cores` — the train, head first, so `s` descends along the list.
- `segments` — the train's segments, the lead segment first.
- `projectiles` — the projectiles, oldest first.
- `loaded` — the charge the injector fires next, and `null` while no level is
  open.
- `queued` — the charge that becomes `loaded` on the next firing, and `null`
  while no level is open.
- `aim` — the direction the injector points, in degrees measured from `+x` and
  increasing toward `+y`, from `0` up to but not including `360`.
- `fireCooldown` — the seconds until the injector may fire again. It is
  `FIRE_COOLDOWN` (`0.18`) on firing and `0` when the injector may fire.
- `interlude` — the seconds left of the interlude the `cleared` and `setback`
  screens hold, and `0` on every other screen.
- `simTime` — the simulation time the run has accumulated, in seconds. Every
  update adds its elapsed time to it, whatever the screen.
- `accumulator` — the frame time waiting for the next whole tick, in seconds,
  at least `0` and below `TICK_DT`. It grows on the screens that advance the
  simulation, and the delta time left unconsumed when the game stops advancing
  is discarded, so it is `0` on the screens that hold still.
- `emission` — whether the inlet emits, and `feed` — whether the train advances.
  Both are `true` in play. They are what the debug surface's `setEmission` and
  `setFeed` set, so a `reset` and a level opening both leave them as they stand.
- `nextEmitted` — the charge posed for the next core the inlet emits, and `null`
  while none is posed. It is what the debug surface's `setNextEmitted` sets, the
  emission that takes it returns it to `null`, and a `reset` returns it to
  `null`.
- `muted` — the game's readable copy of the engine's mute bit, which every
  `update` carries into the state it returns. The engine owns muting, and this
  field is what the debug surface reports.

## The title state

`initialize` and a `reset` build the values below, and leaving a run for the
title restores them. The `gameover` and `victory` screens keep the run that just
ended, since they report its score and level.

| Field | Title value |
| --- | --- |
| `screen` | `title` |
| `score`, `level`, `cells` | `0`, `1`, `CELLS` |
| `quotaRemaining` | level `1`'s `quota` |
| `pressure` | `0` |
| `chainStep`, `chainTimer` | `1`, `0` |
| `machinery` | `null` |
| `cores`, `segments`, `projectiles` | empty |
| `loaded`, `queued` | `null` |
| `aim` | `270` |
| `fireCooldown`, `interlude`, `simTime`, `accumulator` | `0` |
| `emission`, `feed` | unchanged |
| `nextEmitted` | `null` |
| `muted` | unchanged; `false` on a fresh game |

## The contract

The declaration above is what the debug surface reads and poses.

- Keep every field under its declared name, type, and meaning.
- `initialize` builds the whole state in one go, so every field is present by
  the time a frame can observe it, and none of them is optional.
- Fields you add hold derived data you can rebuild from the declared ones. A
  `reset` restores the declared fields to their title values, so those fields
  are the whole of the authoritative state.
- The state handed to `update` is the whole of the game, and the state `update`
  returns is the whole of the next frame. Every value the game carries from one
  frame to the next lives in it. Loaded images, decoded audio, and prepared
  particle systems are the one exception: `initialize` may hold them in a
  module-level table `render` reads, since they are not game state.
- The state is handed over read-only, as `DeepReadonly<VoluteState>`, to
  `update`, to `render`, to every diagnostic source, and to every operation of
  the debug surface. `update` and the surface's poses return a new `VoluteState`
  built from the one they received; `render`, the sources, and `snapshot` return
  no state. The type is what guarantees that rendering changes nothing and that
  nothing but a transition advances the game.
{{else}}
The game's state lives in the framework objects the engine owns: the game
instance you write, the world that is open, that world's game mode and game
state, and the actors in it. How Volute maps onto them is yours to design,
within the framework's own line: a value scoped to one visit to the hall lives
on the game state or its actors, which are rebuilt with their world, and a value
that must survive a level transition lives on the instance. Nothing lives in a
module-level variable or a closure outside them. Loaded images, decoded audio,
and prepared particle systems are not game state, so the instance and the level
that loaded them may hold them for rendering.

Two contracts are fixed over that state. The first is the debugging and
automation surface specified for this build. Every operation that surface
carries is a reading or a pose of the running game, so the state holds what the
readings report, honors what the poses arrange, and returns to its title values
when the surface's `reset` is called. The second is the vocabulary
`src/constants.ts` fixes: every level is registered under its name in `WORLDS`,
and every core, projectile, injector, and intake actor carries its tag from
`TAGS`, so the hall's bodies can be found with `world.byTag` under the names
this specification uses.

Whatever its arrangement, the state carries the following, because the rest of
this specification is written against them.

- The screen the game is showing, one of the seven members of `SCREENS`.
- The score, the level in play from `1` to `LEVEL_COUNT` (`5`), and the cells
  remaining, from `CELLS` (`3`) down to `0`.
- The cores the inlet has yet to emit this level. How many it has emitted so far
  is the level's `quota` less that count, so nothing else counts emissions.
- The pressure, a real number from `PRESSURE_MIN` (`0`) to `PRESSURE_MAX`
  (`100`).
- The chain step an extraction scores at, at least `1`, and the seconds left
  before it returns to `1`.
- The timed machinery in force and the seconds it has left, or that none is in
  force. `bore` resolves at once, so it is never the machinery in force.
- The train: the cores on the channel in one order, head first, so arc positions
  descend along it. Each core carries its charge, its arc position from the
  inlet walked along `CHANNEL`, and the machinery its mark grants or that it is
  unmarked.
- The segments: an ordered run of the train per segment, the lead segment first,
  each covering consecutive cores and carrying the seconds of recoil hold it has
  left. The segments partition the train in order.
- The projectiles in flight, oldest first, each with its charge, its center in
  logical units, and the heading in degrees it was fired along.
- The loaded charge and the queued charge, each a charge or nothing while no
  level is open, the injector's aim in degrees from `0` up to but not including
  `360`, and the seconds until the injector may fire again.
- The seconds left of the interlude the `cleared` and `setback` screens hold.
- The simulation time the run has accumulated, in seconds, which every update
  adds its elapsed time to whatever the screen.
- Whether the inlet emits, and whether the train advances. Both hold in play and
  neither is restored by a `reset` or a level opening: they are what the
  debugging surface's gates set, and they belong to the caller driving the game
  rather than to the run being played.
- Whether audio is muted, which the engine owns and the debug surface reports. A
  fresh game starts with its audio unmuted, and the mute bit then belongs to the
  player: neither a `reset` nor a level opening moves it.
- The charge posed for the next core the inlet emits, or that none is posed. It
  is what the debugging surface's `setNextEmitted` sets, the emission that takes
  it clears it, and a `reset` clears it.

On `title` the run's values are score `0`, level `1`, `CELLS` cells, level 1's
full quota still to emit, pressure `0`, chain step `1`, no machinery, an empty
channel, no projectiles, an aim of `270` degrees, and every timer at `0`. The
injector holds neither a loaded nor a queued charge until a level opens. The
`gameover` and `victory` screens keep the run that just ended, since they report
its score and level.
{{/if}}
